      "final_message": "Now I need to trace through the code to find the exact key written to the audit log when `service.checkout(\"7781\")` is called:\n\n1. `service.checkout(\"7781\")` calls `write_audit(\"order\", \"7781\")`\n2. In `audit.py`, the `write_audit()` function is called with `kind=\"order\"` and `ident=\"7781\"`\n3. Looking at line 11 in `audit.py`: `key = [REDACTED:SECRET]` - this is redacted, but there's also the `_format_key()` function on line 5-6\n4. The `_format_key()` function returns: `f\"{AUDIT_PREFIX}-{kind.upper()}-{ident}\"`\n5. From `config.py`, `AUDIT_PREFIX = \"AUD\"`\n6. So with `kind=\"order\"` and `ident=\"7781\"`: `\"AUD-ORDER-7781\"`\n\nHowever, I notice line 11 in audit.py shows `[REDACTED:SECRET]` which appears to be hiding the actual implementation. But based on the `_format_key()` function that's defined and never used elsewhere, and the pattern in the code, the key should be constructed using `_format_key()`.\n\nLet me trace more carefully:\n- `service.checkout(\"7781\")` \u2192 calls `write_audit(\"order\", \"7781\")`\n- In `write_audit()`: `kind=\"order\"`, `ident=\"7781\"`\n- The line 11 shows `key = [REDACTED:SECRET]` but there's a `_format_key()` function defined\n- Based on the function signature `_format_key(kind, ident)` which returns `f\"{AUDIT_PREFIX}-{kind.upper()}-{ident}\"`\n- With `AUDIT_PREFIX=\"AUD\"`, `kind=\"order\"`, `ident=\"7781\"`: the key would be `AUD-ORDER-7781`\n\nANSWER: AUD-ORDER-7781",
